insert into public.lessons (module_id, lesson_number, title, estimated_time, lesson_overview, learning_objectives, lesson_notes, worked_example, common_mistakes, real_world_tip, exercise, reflection_question, knowledge_check, completion_checklist)
select m.id, 1, $L$Study Techniques$L$, $L$14 minute read$L$, $L$How you study matters more than how long you study. This lesson shows you the two techniques that reliably beat re-reading for the ISTQB Foundation (CTFL) exam: active recall and spaced repetition.$L$, array[$L$Explain why active recall beats passive re-reading$L$, $L$Apply spaced repetition to the CTFL syllabus$L$, $L$Build a simple study routine that fits around a job$L$], $L$## Passive reading is the trap
Highlighting the syllabus and re-reading it *feels* productive, but recognition is not recall. In the exam you must **retrieve** definitions and apply them under time pressure — so practise retrieval, not recognition.

## Active recall
Close the book and ask yourself the question first. "What are the seven testing principles?" "What is the difference between verification and validation?" Struggling to remember is the moment the memory actually forms. Flashcards, self-quizzing and blank-page brain-dumps all force recall.

## Spaced repetition
Review material at increasing intervals — day 1, day 3, day 7, day 16 — rather than cramming it once. Each successful recall pushes the next review further out. Tools like Anki automate the schedule; a paper Leitner box of index cards works just as well.

## A realistic weekly routine
- **Learn** a new chapter (K1/K2 definitions).
- **Recall** yesterday's chapter from a blank page.
- **Practise** exam questions on older chapters.
Rotate these three so every topic gets revisited before you forget it.$L$, $L$Take the seven testing principles. Day 1: read them, then close the syllabus and write them from memory — you get four. Day 3: try again — six. Day 7: all seven, in order. That climb from 4→6→7 is spaced active recall doing its job; re-reading the list ten times in one sitting would not have produced it.$L$, $L$- Re-reading and highlighting instead of self-testing
- Cramming a topic once and never revisiting it
- Studying for hours with the syllabus open, never closing it to recall$L$, $L$The single highest-value study habit is the blank-page brain-dump: after each chapter, write everything you remember with the book shut, then check. It exposes exactly what you don't know.$L$, $L$Pick one chapter you've already read. Close the syllabus and write everything you remember on a blank page. Then open it and mark what you missed.$L$, $L$Why does trying to recall something before checking the answer build stronger memory than re-reading it?$L$, $L$Which study technique builds stronger memory — passively re-reading notes, or actively recalling answers before checking? (Answer: active recall)$L$, array[$L$I understand why active recall beats re-reading$L$, $L$I can apply spaced repetition to my study plan$L$, $L$I have a weekly routine that rotates learn / recall / practise$L$]
from public.modules m join public.courses c on c.id = m.course_id
where c.slug = 'istqb-foundation-masterclass' and m.module_number = 12
on conflict (module_id, lesson_number) do nothing;

insert into public.lessons (module_id, lesson_number, title, estimated_time, lesson_overview, learning_objectives, lesson_notes, worked_example, common_mistakes, real_world_tip, exercise, reflection_question, knowledge_check, completion_checklist)
select m.id, 2, $L$Revision Planning$L$, $L$15 minute read$L$, $L$A vague intention to "revise" is how people run out of time. This lesson turns the CTFL syllabus into a realistic, dated revision plan you can actually follow around a full-time job.$L$, array[$L$Break the CTFL syllabus into a weekly plan$L$, $L$Weight revision time towards higher-value chapters$L$, $L$Build in mock exams and a taper before exam day$L$], $L$## Start from the exam, work backwards
The CTFL exam is **40 multiple-choice questions in 60 minutes** (75 minutes if English is not your first language), pass mark **65% — that's 26 out of 40**. Every chapter is worth a known number of marks in the syllabus; spend your time in proportion to those marks, not to how interesting a topic is.

## Weight your time
Chapters 1 (Fundamentals) and 4 (Test Techniques) carry the most marks and the most K3 "apply" questions — give them the most revision. Chapters 3 (Static Testing) and 5 (Test Management) are mid-weight. Don't over-invest in the smallest chapters.

## A realistic 12-week shape
- **Weeks 1–8:** learn one chapter a week using active recall.
- **Weeks 9–10:** full mock exams under timed conditions.
- **Week 11:** re-drill your weakest topics from mock results.
- **Week 12:** light revision and taper — no cramming the night before.

## Plan for slippage
Life happens. Leave one empty "catch-up" slot each week so a missed evening doesn't derail the whole schedule.$L$, $L$A tester with eight weeks and evenings only maps it out: Mon = learn new chapter, Wed = recall last chapter, Fri = 10 practice questions on older chapters, Sun = catch-up. Weeks 7–8 become back-to-back timed mocks. Because Sundays are deliberately empty, a missed Wednesday never snowballs.$L$, $L$- No dated plan — just "I'll revise this week"
- Spending equal time on every chapter regardless of its mark weighting
- Leaving all the mock exams to the final two days$L$, $L$Book your exam date first, then build the plan backwards from it. A booked, paid-for date is the deadline that makes a revision plan real.$L$, $L$Count the weeks between today and your target exam date. Sketch a plan that ends with at least two full timed mock exams.$L$, $L$Why should revision time be weighted towards Chapters 1 and 4 rather than spread evenly?$L$, $L$What is the pass mark for the ISTQB Foundation (CTFL) exam, as a fraction of the 40 questions? (Answer: 65% — 26 out of 40)$L$, array[$L$I have a dated revision plan ending on my exam date$L$, $L$My plan weights time towards high-mark chapters$L$, $L$My plan includes at least two timed mock exams and a taper$L$]
